A||N||6||kornholio||filling up the gas tank||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||01-04-2000||06:32 AM||||How much more gas can I put in my car after the pump clicks telling me it's full?  I have an 86 SE V6.  Is there any damage done to the floater thing in the tank if I fill it too much?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shiner||01-04-2000||10:37 AM||||Sometimes I find myself doing the "manly" thing (topping off the tank) after the pump clicks.  You know what happens?  You get gas on your shoes.  But it's okay, because when you walk into the room, everyone will know you are a manly mechanic from the smell.||||reg||6
Z||000002||fiero56||01-04-2000||11:33 AM||||I've found it depends on which gas station you go to. The guy I bought my Fiero from was telling me, every 8,000 miles it gets a fuel filter... eventually one of his lasts comments was "she ALWAYS get Shell gasoline". The guy almost had a tear in his eye as I was handing over the cash. Well, I've found when I go to Shell, I can give it another $2.50 or so. The Fiero always goes to shell. But when filling up a friends car at a different station, topping it up after $.25 we had gas spewing everywhere. When we took it to Shell later that week, we got an extra $2.50 in. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||01-04-2000||01:04 PM||||I always round up to an even number when I'm pumping my gas, but really it's not good for the environment. Something about putting extra fuel in after the pump clicks off causes more hydrocarbons and ozone to be vented into the air. I thought adding more ozone would be good, but not at earth level. Ozone is only good in the upper atmosphere where it helps block UV radiation.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spyder||01-04-2000||01:28 PM||||I was told to stop when it clicks. You might end up with a vapor canister full of fuel.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dave85||01-04-2000||04:16 PM||||I've put in as much as $4.00 after the click. My gas gauge won't read completly full if I stop at the click. I FILL my tank about 95% of the time and never had any ill effects. Plus it gives me an extra couple days before filling again.     ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-04-2000||08:31 PM||||this can vary from station to station and ever from one pump to the next at the same station. <P>There are several types of nozle on the market. All stations use one of these few, but diferances in pump pressure, nozle tip, and several ofter things make it somewhat random. Even the angle of rotation of the handle of the nozle makes a diferance.<P>For example, at some stations I just hand the nozle in the tank set the handle to full speed and go wash windows. In other stations I have to rotate the handle some or the thing won't even run on slow.||||reg||1
